{'name': 'new_set_first_trial_yes_gap', 'description': 'Yes rate on non-example targets on the first trial after the set changes (or session start, trial_index 0) minus yes rate on subsequent trials with the same set as the previous trial; nonzero observed vs a null near the stimulus-driven value means a set-novelty effect the stationary model lacks (positive = more liberal on the first query about a new set).', 't_observed': -0.23984300013711782, 'null_mean': -0.20850922002833766, 'null_std': 0.015445279479176314, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': -2.0286962208113546, 'p_value': 0.03796203796203796, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.9820179820179821, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': True, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 0.3036963036963037, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'small_rule_nonmember_in_range_yes', 'description': 'Among sets of 2+ distinct examples that all fit some rule with <=20 members (squares, cubes, powers, multiples of 5-12, ends in d, ...), yes rate on non-example targets that are NOT in the smallest such rule and lie strictly between the smallest and largest example; observed below null means people reject rule-violating in-range targets more firmly than the model (its intervals/noise over-produce yes there), above null means people fill the range more.', 't_observed': 0.05034965034965035, 'null_mean': 0.05416853146853147, 'null_std': 0.006200651586867018, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': -0.6158838414610341, 'p_value': 0.5974025974025974, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.7472527472527473, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'small_rule_member_yes', 'description': 'Among sets of 2+ distinct examples that all fit some rule with <=20 members, yes rate on non-example targets that ARE in the smallest such rule (anywhere on the number line); observed above null means the model is under-confident in clear rules (too much mass on intervals/noise/competitors), below null means it over-generalises the rule.', 't_observed': 0.8777777777777778, 'null_mean': 0.8680249999999999, 'null_std': 0.01939356020613894, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': 0.5028874365569369, 'p_value': 0.6653346653346653, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.33266733266733267, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'broad_rule_nonmember_yes', 'description': 'Among sets of 2+ distinct examples whose smallest consistent rule has 21-50 members (e.g. odd, even, multiples of 3 or 4, primes), yes rate on non-example targets outside that rule; observed below null means people respect broad rules (parity, multiples of 3) more strictly than the model, above null means people ignore them more.', 't_observed': 0.159375, 'null_mean': 0.15390833333333334, 'null_std': 0.011728468007611036, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': 0.466102364189349, 'p_value': 0.6413586413586414, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.3206793206793207, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'single_example_divisor_multiple_bonus', 'description': 'For single-example sets with example >= 2, yes rate on non-example targets that divide or are a multiple of the example minus yes rate on other targets; observed above null means people use multiplicative (divisor/multiple) relations from one example more than the model (which lacks "divisors of n" concepts), below null means less.', 't_observed': -0.012444444444444452, 'null_mean': 0.014171555555555552, 'null_std': 0.05733878872236618, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': -0.46418838962354786, 'p_value': 0.6553446553446554, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.6783216783216783, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'cell_unanimous_no_rate', 'description': 'Share of (set, target) cells (with >=5 responses) in which every response is "no"; observed above null means people reject more cells with total unanimity than the model\'s graded probabilities allow (the model spreads too much yes-probability over cells that people treat as clear non-members), below null the reverse.', 't_observed': 0.5078888054094666, 'null_mean': 0.5085905334335087, 'null_std': 0.009054563578447703, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': -0.07749992784990938, 'p_value': 0.985014985014985, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.5444555444555444, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'ascending_order_generalization_gap', 'description': 'For sets of 3+ examples, yes rate on non-example targets when the examples were shown in ascending order minus when shown in non-ascending order; nonzero observed vs null means presentation order changes generalisation (positive = ordered sequences invite broader, rule-like generalisation), which the order-blind model can only produce through set content.', 't_observed': 0.042753795873902045, 'null_mean': 0.04356345067066113, 'null_std': 0.01843357321249614, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': -0.043922835113173624, 'p_value': 0.955044955044955, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.5234765234765235, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}
{'name': 'cell_unanimous_yes_rate', 'description': 'Share of (set, target) cells (with >=5 responses) in which every response is "yes"; observed above null means the model under-produces confident, unanimous endorsements (its yes-probabilities for clear members are too far below 1), below null means it over-produces them.', 't_observed': 0.10894064613072878, 'null_mean': 0.10887302779864765, 'null_std': 0.0055534260772090215, 'n_replicates': 1000, 'z_score': 0.012175966897017696, 'p_value': 1.0, 'p_value_one_sided': 0.5174825174825175, 'p_value_is_floor': False, 'significant': False, 'error': None, 'p_value_fdr': 1.0, 'significant_fdr': False}

